WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.340
Korean auto insurers face a 107.6% loss ratio after Tesla sales tripled. This is Tesla Shorts Time,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:17.700
episode 638. Korean insurers, according to the Korea Times, calculate that figure as claims paid against premiums collected on electric vehicles or EVs.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:28.540
The Tesla boom has raised concerns over those auto insurers underwriting results. A loss ratio above 100% means claims are already outrunning the premiums meant to cover them.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:33.680
That mismatch sits between how policies are priced and what repairs and claims actually

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.960
cost on a fast-growing vehicle segment.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:41.600
Higher vehicle counts alone do not automatically improve underwriting when parts, labor and

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.000
battery replacement expenses stay elevated.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.780
The data forces a closer look at whether current actuarial models treat advanced driver assistance

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.880
hardware and high voltage systems as standard equipment or as recurring cost drivers.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.560
Repair networks must stock specialized high-voltage components and train technicians on structural

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.260
battery packs that differ from conventional vehicles.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.940
Claim severity therefore rises faster than volume can offset it through simple diversification.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:14.320
Tesla's vertical integration of battery packs and software gives the company direct visibility

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.020
into failure modes that ensures must-price without equivalent data.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.940
Ensures then face a choice between raising premiums across the segment or carving out exclusions

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.420
that limit coverage for assisted driving incidents.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.440
Both of those choices alter the total cost of ownership for buyers.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.800
The Korean market is an early window into the dynamic because Tesla's growth there has

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.260
been rapid and concentrated.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.280
Without shared diagnostic data or over-the-air mitigation of common failure points, external

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.080
insurers lack the levers Tesla itself can apply to future vehicles.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:52.360
Over time, the same integration that raises short-term claim costs can compress them if

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:57.940
over-the-air updates reduce crash frequency, or if shared diagnostic data lowers diagnostic

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.360
labor.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.520
The Korean numbers function less as a verdict on Tesla, and more as an early indicator

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.260
of how quickly the insurance industry must adapt its risk models to vehicles who software

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:10.320
and hardware keep changing after delivery.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.140
Until those models catch up, loss ratios above 100% will remain a predictable feature

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.860
of rapid EV adoption rather than an anomaly.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.680
Currently, markets with slower Tesla penetration may avoid the same pressure until volumes

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.280
reach similar thresholds.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.640
Actuaries will need new data streams on software to find safety features and battery degradation

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.900
curves to keep pace with hardware that evolves after purchase.

